1. What is a Binary Tree?

A Binary Tree is a non-linear data structure in which each node can have at most two children, referred to as the left child and the right child.
Binary trees are used to represent hierarchical relationships.

2. Basic Terminology

Node
The basic unit of a tree that contains data.

Root
The topmost node of the tree.

Parent
A node that has one or more children.

Child
A node that descends from a parent node.

Leaf Node
A node with no children.

Internal Node
A node with at least one child.

Edge
The connection between two nodes.

Height
The maximum number of edges from a node to the deepest leaf.

Depth
The number of edges from the root to a node.

Level
Depth + 1

3. Structure of a Binary Tree

Each node contains:

Data
Reference to left child
Reference to right child

4. Types of Binary Trees

Full Binary Tree:
Every node has either 0 or 2 children.
No node has only one child.

Perfect Binary Tree:
All internal nodes have exactly 2 children and all leaf nodes are at the same level.
Total nodes = 2^h − 1

Complete Binary Tree:
All levels are completely filled except possibly the last level.
The last level is filled from left to right.
Used in Heap.

Skewed Binary Tree:
Every node has only one child.
Can be left-skewed or right-skewed.
Worst case for performance.

Balanced Binary Tree:
The height difference between left and right subtree is not more than 1.
Improves efficiency.

5. Binary Tree vs Binary Search Tree (BST)

Binary Tree

No ordering rule
Searching is slow

Binary Search Tree

Left subtree contains smaller values
Right subtree contains greater values
Searching is fast

6. Tree Traversals

Traversal means visiting all nodes of the tree.

Preorder Traversal
Root → Left → Right
Used for copying trees and prefix expressions.

Inorder Traversal
Left → Root → Right
In BST, inorder traversal gives sorted order.

Postorder Traversal
Left → Right → Root
Used for deleting trees and expression evaluation.

Level Order Traversal
Traverses level by level using Queue.
Also called Breadth First Search (BFS).

7. Height of Binary Tree

Height is the maximum number of edges from the root to the deepest leaf node.

Worst case time complexity: O(n)
Balanced tree time complexity: O(log n)

8. Properties of Binary Tree

Maximum nodes at level L = 2^L
Maximum nodes in a binary tree of height H = 2^(H+1) − 1
Minimum height for N nodes = log₂(N)
Number of edges = Number of nodes − 1

9. Representation of Binary Tree

Linked Representation
Each node contains data and references to left and right child.

Array Representation
Used in Complete Binary Tree.

Index relationships:
Parent = (i − 1) / 2
Left child = 2i + 1
Right child = 2i + 2

